WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our first guest tonight
is a Peabody-winning comedian

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.604
and actor you know
from "Patriot Act,"

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.405
"The Daily Show"
and "The Morning Show."

00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:08.641
His comedy special,
"The King's Jester,"

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:10.776
is streaming now on Netflix.
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:13.480
-In the Middle East,
Indians, Pakistanis, Bengalis,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.381
Sri Lankans, we're the help.

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:17.116
Right? We're the Mexicans
of the Middle East.

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:18.384
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:20.153
Even Brown people
have their own Brown people.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:22.454
That's how those buildings
got there!

00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:24.590
The only people that love to
[bleep] on Indians

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.893
more than the Saudis
are the Brits.

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.061
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:29.129
The only difference is,

00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:31.431
we love when the Brits
[bleep] on us.

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:32.765
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:34.700
There are Indian uncles
here tonight that are like,

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.202
"Wow! London decor!"

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:37.470
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.640
-Please welcome back to the show
Hasan Minhaj, everybody!

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.141
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:52.151 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.151 --> 00:01:02.161 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:04.397 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:07.300
-Welcome back to the show.
-Thank you!

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.736
-Congratulations
on the new special.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:11.070
It must be so great
to have it out.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.007
-Yeah, it's a huge relief
to just get it out.

00:01:15.007 --> 00:01:16.476
I --

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:17.810
We've seen each other recently.
-We have.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.412
We were at a --
We did a charity event

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:22.348
for the Natural Resources
Defense Council -- NRDC.

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:24.717
-Yes, NRDC.
Not the Department of Defense.

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.318
It sounds like that.
-It does sound like that.

00:01:26.318 --> 00:01:28.688
-Yeah, it's these weird --

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.356
People don't know this --
we're in the Illuminati,

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:31.958
and we do these --

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:34.294
we do these gigs for,
like, billionaires,

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.763
and we all know
billionaires love, um...

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:39.899
saving the environment
and tax loopholes.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:40.967
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:42.101
When you can have the same --

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:43.303
When they have both
in the same night...

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:44.904
-Yeah, just, mm!

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:48.340
And then you get the call from
Seth, and it's a stacked lineup.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.909
-It was a great lineup
that night.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:51.844
-And everybody bombs.
-Yeah.

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:54.113
-Like,
you do your best material,

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:57.483
and the room is always like,
it's the founder of Uber,

00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:00.753
Condoleezza Rice and the ghost
of Winston Churchill.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:01.988
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:04.123
And everybody
is just shooting air balls.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.658
Like, you know how hard it is
when you're just

00:02:06.658 --> 00:02:09.728
giving your A material,
and they're just like, "Mm."

00:02:09.728 --> 00:02:11.864
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:11.864 --> 00:02:14.367
And then, when you angrily yell,
"You're not better than me!"

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:17.770
and they're like, "Actually..."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.272
-"I own the six media companies
that run the world."

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:22.709
You're like,
"Please, pick up my pilot."

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.544
Yeah, it's good.
-You --

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.145
We were talking backstage
at that event.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:27.646
You have two kids now.
-Yes.

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:30.149
-How have you found fatherhood
in the early years?

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:32.886
-It's so crazy, because,
for years, my wife and I,

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:35.154
we were trying to have kids,
and then we finally had kids.

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:39.458
And now, we have a daughter
that's 4 and a son that's 2.

00:02:39.458 --> 00:02:43.062
And we just got
pushed out of the city.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.096
We were
in a two-bedroom apartment.

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:46.165
I was in Hell's Kitchen
for years.

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:48.467
And then, when my son was born,
I was just asleep

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:50.136
in the living room
with my mother-in-law,

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:52.071
and I'm like,
"I didn't think my comedy career

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:55.274
was supposed to start --
end where it started."

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:56.542
You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:57.843
Like, I've worked
too hard for this.

00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:00.412
So we moved to Connecticut,
and...

00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:01.881
I can feel you judging me.

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:04.250
[ Laughter ]
I know everybody's judging me.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.085
We live in Greenwich,
Connecticut, which is like,

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.887
"Get Out" white.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:10.589
-Like, everybody there
either works at a hedge fund

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:12.692
or is a war criminal.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:15.061
Like, sometimes a little bit
of both, right?

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:18.163
And -- And every morning,
I just see all the --

00:03:18.163 --> 00:03:22.000
you know, the bankers and
traders in Patagonia puffers.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.337
And my daughter will be like,
"Who are they?"

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.305
I'm like,
"Look, babe, it's criminals."

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:28.073
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:29.942
"They're the reason
daddy doesn't have a pension.

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:32.445
You know, it's them."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.013
But it's so weird.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:36.082
You get to do parent-teacher
conference now, right?

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:38.384
-Yep, it's very nice to be
in person and do them again.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.986
-Is it nerve-racking for you?
-It is very nerve-racking to me.

00:03:40.986 --> 00:03:43.523
They often tell me hard truths
about my kids

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:46.592
that I already knew.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:49.595
-For me, it's -- has it hit you
that you're a parent?

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:52.532
Because for me, we had our first
parent-teacher conference,

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:55.634
in person, and I remember
they were saying all this stuff

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.237
about my daughter,
and I was like,

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:59.539
"What are mom and dad
gonna think?"

00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:02.975
And they were like,
"You're mom and dad."

00:04:02.975 --> 00:04:04.844
And they gave us this envelope,
and I was like,

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:07.280
"Beena, we gotta hide this.
Like, we can't --

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.581
Yeah, we don't want mom and --"
Yeah.

00:04:09.581 --> 00:04:13.051
That's still in me, where I'm
37, but it's still like I'm 27.

00:04:13.051 --> 00:04:15.555
Sometimes, me and Beena will be
holding hands in public,

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:17.790
and if we see
Indian aunties or uncles,

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.994
we'll just drop hands
and Euro step around them,

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:23.496
and then hold hands again.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:25.464
I'm like, "They can't tell
people in the community

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:26.665
that we're dating."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:29.836
"What am I doing?"
-One of the things I enjoy

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:32.438
about being a dad
is reading books to my kids.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:33.672
-Yes.
-Are you enjoying that part?

00:04:33.672 --> 00:04:37.242
-Okay, so one of the things
I love doing now

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:38.410
is reading with the kids,

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.779
because we're making
new memories.

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:42.881
I remember growing up,
I wanted my dad to read to me.

00:04:42.881 --> 00:04:44.317
I was like,
"Will you read to me?"

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:46.452
And he's like, "I send you to
school, so you read to me."

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:47.987
-Oh, interesting.
-Yeah!

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:51.356
So, like, now I'll be like,
"All right, let's --

00:04:51.356 --> 00:04:52.391
let's read together."

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:55.294
Now, are you an old-book
or new-book guy?

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:58.430
-We are old-book people,
which I'm trying to change.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.733
I appreciate there's a lot of
great authors right now.

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.434
-Yourself included.
-Thank you very much.

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:03.902
Thank you for the mention.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:07.940
-Yes. But the old books
are terrifying.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.044
And the new books
are just kind of weird.

00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:13.946
All right, so the old books,
we're doing all the classics.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.782
You know, we're doing
"Cat in the Hat,"

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:18.351
and then you're like,
"Oh, my God.

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:20.353
This drug-induced cat
on ketamine..."

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:22.722
-Yep.
-"...bursts in to their house,

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:25.858
two children, alone,
destroys it,

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.193
with Thing One and Thing Two."

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:29.495
That probably offends somebody.
-Yep.

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.563
-Really, they're just things?
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:31.563 --> 00:05:33.899
Within one page, they sober up,

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:36.169
clean up the whole place,
they leave.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:38.070
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.572
"Where the Wild Things Are"!
-Yeah.

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:41.173
-All right,
these negligent parents

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:44.210
send their kid upstairs
with nothing to eat.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:48.113
He has to go across the ocean,
befriend monsters,

00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:50.249
and then finally
comes back to his family.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:51.183
Horrible.

00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:53.019
Then there's "The Giving Tree."

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:54.887
-Yeah, that's a rough one.
-Just really --

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.655
That's just like
"humanity is awful."

00:05:56.655 --> 00:05:57.990
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.859
-"Don't trust anybody.
They will destroy everything."

00:06:00.859 --> 00:06:02.528
And so I was like, "All right,
let's try the new books."

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:03.896
-Yeah, you brought one.
-So we got gifted this.

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:04.964
-Okay, this was a gift.

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:06.498
-All right,
this was a gift we got.

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:07.767
-Okay.
"Little Feminist."

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.201
-"Little Feminist."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:10.569
Great message.

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:13.339
I think we're all on board.
-Yeah.

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:15.474
-Most of the country.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.976
And I'm reading it
to my daughter, and she goes,

00:06:17.976 --> 00:06:19.845
"Oh, my God!
Like, Daddy, who's that?"

00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:22.115
I go,
"That's Queen Elizabeth I."

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:24.317
She goes, "That's amazing."

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:26.051
And I go --
she goes, "Who's that?"

00:06:26.051 --> 00:06:27.986
I go, "That's Sacagawea."

00:06:27.986 --> 00:06:29.922
And she goes,
"Daddy, are they friends?"

00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:31.924
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:35.794
And I was like, "Are they..."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.794 --> 00:06:39.565
"Well...no...
No."

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:42.368
She goes, "Why?"

00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:47.740
And I go,
"Well, one could argue...

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.876
she's dead because of her."

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:51.444
[ Laughter ]

00:06:51.444 --> 00:06:52.978
Then she was like,
"All right, who's that?"

00:06:52.978 --> 00:06:54.112
She saw this little Brown girl
in the corner.

00:06:54.112 --> 00:06:55.814
I go, "That's Malala!"

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:57.750
And she goes,
"Are they friends?"

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.184
[ Laughter ]

00:06:59.184 --> 00:07:00.686
And I was like,
"Absolutely not."

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:02.454
[ Laughter ]

00:07:02.454 --> 00:07:04.490
And she goes, "Well, why?"

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:08.761
And I go, "Well, because Malala
is from Pakistan."

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:11.397
And she goes,
"Well, why is that a problem?"

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:13.432
And I go,
"Well, one could argue..."

00:07:13.432 --> 00:07:15.101
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:17.336
"...Pakistan's a country
because of her."

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:18.537
[ Laughter ]

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:20.873
And she was like, "Cool!"
I'm like, "Not cool."

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:22.174
[ Laughter ]

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:24.143
"You know what?
Let's just, um --

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:25.711
Let's just read
'The Giving Tree.'"

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:26.845
[ Laughter ]

00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:28.113
-"The Giving Tree"
is maybe less triggering.

00:07:28.113 --> 00:07:29.248
I've got a lot more to ask you.

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:30.282
We'll be right back with more

00:07:30.282 --> 00:07:31.717
from Hasan Minhaj, everybody.

